Kompakt: the notification LED takes brightness and the lights HAL's timed nodes

The red, green and blue sinks of the MT6370 RGB driver (the old
implementation, which the MT6370 uses; the "*2" one is the MT6372's) now take
the brightness they are given, and carry the six nodes the LineageOS lights
HAL drives a QCOM LPG through, so its notification light (per-app colours,
brightness, pulse speed) runs on the PMIC with no app in between.

Brightness: the ODM's bright_set wrote level 1 whatever was asked. The
current field is ISINK bits 2:0, level n = 4 mA * n, 1 to 6 (mainline
leds-mt6370-rgb: LINEAR_RANGE(4000, 1, 6, 4000)); max_brightness was already
6 and stays. Level 1 is what every light used before. The ODM also rewrote
the PWM dimming field (bits 5:3) to 7 (1 kHz) on every brightness write;
that is now the probe value instead, so pwm_mode still shows a steady light,
and a frequency chosen through pwm_dim_freq or blink_set is no longer
overwritten by the next brightness write. LED4 (charger indicator) keeps
exactly the ODM behaviour. A brightness read while the shared enable work is
pending returns the stored level; it used to read the not yet enabled sink
as off and zero the level the work was about to enable.

Timed nodes (static, 0664, on red, green and blue; vendor init chowns them
to system): blink, start_idx, duty_pcts, pause_lo, pause_hi, ramp_step_ms.
blink=1 programs the breath registers and arms breath mode through the same
register write the breath_mode trigger uses; blink=0 returns the channel to
constant current (cc_mode), a solid light at its level. The HAL's static
path writes blink=0 before the brightness, so a solid light is cc, not pwm.

  rise = fall = ramp_step_ms * number of duty_pcts entries, split over the
                two segments (tr1 = tr2, tf1 = tf2): 125 ms + 200 ms * n
  hold        = pause_hi, ton: 125 ms + 200 ms * n
  pause       = pause_lo, toff: 250 ms + 400 ms * n          (n = 0..15)

Nearest code, clamped. The level is the channel's brightness, or, when that
is 0 (the HAL's timed path writes brightness 0 first), the peak of duty_pcts
as a percentage of max_brightness, rounded up. duty_pcts is otherwise a QCOM
ramp table this PMIC cannot play; start_idx indexes QCOM's shared table and
is stored and ignored. Until a timing node is written, blink=1 keeps the
driver's default timing (the HAL's hardware flash mode).

Phase: the channel's enable bit is cleared before breath is armed, and the
brightness write schedules the shared enable work, which switches every lit
channel on in one write 100 ms after the last change. The HAL writes
brightness 0 to each channel before its pattern, which now disables it at
once (the forced level 1 had sent off through the delayed work as well), so
a channel lit across a colour change restarts with the others instead of
breathing out of phase.

